                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A 'true' visual story where you have to make multiple decisions - which will decide the route and the end of the story. Set in a early industrial age (very like 19th century Europe) but with magic as the thriving force.  The story itself is a bit of a mix of a youth light novel and some more serious  thoughts about society.\r\nVisual design is well made and the music-themes are really great. Try to explore the possibilities yourself - it will shurly take more time to figure out how to change the story by yourself, but it is definitly more fun then looking up in some guide.  Don't worry you can play casual and you have unlimited save-points (Mention this, because recently i have seen otherwise on a different visual novel). When you look out for it, you will even get some basic introduction in psychology and the basics on digital electronics.\r\nAfter all that positive said now some critic points:\r\nSome story-endings are much, much shorter then others. This feels a bit unbalanced from a game playing experience. Especially when you really can imagine a continuation on this storylines. I would really like to see a sequel to continue with this storylines.\r\nDespite the general good quality of the visuals, in one of the storyline you will explore some art which doesn't match the style, especially for some characters. Also i personally think that in this storyline some of the in-story-logic feelt a bit off. I can comprehend the underlying principles and ideas but they aren't so well presented and wrapped in a captivating, consistent story as in the others. So this feels a bit forced. I can't help but speculate: Is this do to the pressure of running out of time and budget or a creator leaving the team or was this forced by one of the sponsors (a ministry in indonesia - when you look up their history of student backed protest, you may understand my thoughts)\r\nAnyway: worth buying and the time playing.(Got it with ~80% discount)",
